1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,438
Production is the live system that real
users depend on, and a commit is one saved

2
00:00:08,478 --> 00:00:12,367
snapshot of the project. The textbook's
comment on that question is the lesson of

3
00:00:12,407 --> 00:00:16,248
this video: the answer isn't in Git. It's
in GitHub objects: an environment, its

4
00:00:16,288 --> 00:00:19,935
protection rules, a deployment record.
If you can't say which rules gate the

5
00:00:19,975 --> 00:00:23,428
production job and who can bypass
them, you don't control your releases.

6
00:00:23,768 --> 00:00:28,679
And here's how a team finds out that it
doesn't. The workflow file says environment:

7
00:00:28,719 --> 00:00:32,157
production. Everyone has read
that line and assumed a gate.

8
00:00:32,197 --> 00:00:36,165
One day a deployment goes to production
without anyone approving it.

9
00:00:36,205 --> 00:00:40,056
The YAML was correct. So what was
missing? Take a guess, out loud.

10
00:00:44,896 --> 00:00:49,702
The gate had never been configured, and
naming an environment that doesn't exist

11
00:00:49,742 --> 00:00:53,337
creates it, without any rule.
So what does make a real gate?

12
00:00:53,377 --> 00:00:58,425
Three things together, and before this video
ends you'll be able to say all three to

13
00:00:58,465 --> 00:00:58,789
a CTO.

14
00:01:00,629 --> 00:01:04,435
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:09,354
A workflow is a file that tells GitHub
Actions which jobs to run when an event

16
00:01:09,394 --> 00:01:13,831
happens, and a job is a list of steps
on one fresh machine, the runner.

17
00:01:13,871 --> 00:01:19,066
Chapter 20A taught the parts of a workflow.
Chapter 20B uses those parts to deliver

18
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:21,462
software, and this is its first video.

19
00:01:21,802 --> 00:01:26,108
The textbook is firm about the layers
here. Nothing in this chapter is a Git

20
00:01:26,148 --> 00:01:29,195
feature. Git supplies the
commit, the ref and the tag.

21
00:01:29,235 --> 00:01:33,255
GitHub Actions decides when to run,
on which machine, with which token.

22
00:01:33,295 --> 00:01:36,515
And GitHub decides what a
"deployment" and a "check" are.

23
00:01:36,855 --> 00:01:41,929
Three topics. Environments: what they are,
what their rules do, and what you must

24
00:01:41,969 --> 00:01:46,853
configure outside the workflow file.
Workflow 8, which deploys to staging, the

25
00:01:46,893 --> 00:01:51,778
rehearsal system, what was built once.
And workflow 9, which promotes the same

26
00:01:51,818 --> 00:01:57,018
artifact to production behind an approval.
An artifact is a set of files that a job

27
00:01:57,058 --> 00:01:58,155
uploads to GitHub.

28
00:01:58,495 --> 00:02:03,081
Git still earns its place in the
demonstration, because three questions about a

29
00:02:03,121 --> 00:02:06,302
deployment are Git questions.
Are these the same bytes?

30
00:02:06,342 --> 00:02:10,635
Which commits am I about to ship? And
is this deployment going forwards or

31
00:02:10,675 --> 00:02:11,220
backwards?

32
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:15,834
The deployments in the labs are simulated.
The script prints what it would deploy

33
00:02:15,874 --> 00:02:17,378
and changes nothing anywhere.

34
00:02:19,218 --> 00:02:20,796
Here's what you'll walk away with.

35
00:02:21,136 --> 00:02:22,548
After this video you can:

36
00:02:22,888 --> 00:02:27,769
list what must be configured on GitHub,
outside the workflow file, for environment:

37
00:02:27,809 --> 00:02:28,954
to protect anything;

38
00:02:29,294 --> 00:02:33,864
explain required reviewers, deployment
branch rules and environment secrets;

39
00:02:34,204 --> 00:02:39,303
explain workflow 8 line by line and say
which commit range a deployment contains;

40
00:02:39,643 --> 00:02:44,188
explain workflow 9 and what "the bytes
that were tested are the bytes that ship"

41
00:02:44,228 --> 00:02:44,704
requires;

42
00:02:45,044 --> 00:02:47,375
guard a deployment
against going backwards.

43
00:02:49,215 --> 00:02:54,497
In one sentence. An environment is a named
GitHub object that a job can reference,

44
00:02:54,537 --> 00:02:59,884
and that holds protection rules the job
must pass before it starts, and secrets and

45
00:02:59,924 --> 00:03:05,335
variables the job can read only after that.
A secret is an encrypted value stored in

46
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:06,503
GitHub's settings.

47
00:03:06,843 --> 00:03:11,955
Precisely. A job references an environment
with the environment key, either as a

48
00:03:11,995 --> 00:03:15,432
name or as a mapping with a
name and a URL. The rules:

49
00:03:15,772 --> 00:03:19,501
Required reviewers: the job waits until
a listed person or team approves.

50
00:03:19,541 --> 00:03:23,683
Up to six users or teams. One approval is
enough. And "prevent self-review" stops

51
00:03:23,723 --> 00:03:26,212
the person who started the
run from approving it.

52
00:03:26,552 --> 00:03:32,359
Wait timer: the job waits a fixed time
after it is triggered, from 1 to 43,200

53
00:03:32,399 --> 00:03:35,957
minutes, which is 30 days.
Waiting isn't billed.

54
00:03:36,297 --> 00:03:40,298
Deployment branches and tags: only
runs on matching refs may deploy.

55
00:03:40,338 --> 00:03:45,172
The choices are "No restriction", "Protected
branches only", or "Selected branches

56
00:03:45,212 --> 00:03:50,046
and tags". Patterns are matched against
the ref, and a star doesn't match a slash.

57
00:03:50,386 --> 00:03:54,746
Allow administrators to bypass: lets
a repository administrator force the

58
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:59,026
deployment. It's on by default and
can be switched off per environment.

59
00:03:59,366 --> 00:04:03,737
Custom deployment protection rules, where
a GitHub App decides: public preview.

60
00:04:04,077 --> 00:04:08,587
Environment secrets: secrets that
exist only for jobs that reference the

61
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:13,264
environment, and that aren't readable
before a required approval is given.

62
00:04:13,304 --> 00:04:16,866
Environment variables: the
same scoping, without secrecy.

63
00:04:17,206 --> 00:04:19,708
Two details decide how safe this is.

64
00:04:20,048 --> 00:04:25,079
First: all protection rules must pass before
a job that references the environment

65
00:04:25,119 --> 00:04:28,603
is sent to a runner. The gate
is in front of the machine.

66
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:32,004
It's not a step inside the
job that the job could skip.

67
00:04:32,344 --> 00:04:37,551
Quick quiz. A workflow names an environment
with a typing error in the name.

68
00:04:37,591 --> 00:04:43,074
What happens? A, the run stops with an
error. B, the job starts, with no gate at

69
00:04:43,114 --> 00:04:44,248
all. Your answer?

70
00:04:49,088 --> 00:04:53,882
B, and that's the second detail. A workflow
that names an environment that doesn't

71
00:04:53,922 --> 00:04:58,835
exist creates it, without any rule. Anyone
who can edit workflows can do that, while

72
00:04:58,875 --> 00:05:02,372
only repository administrators
can configure an environment.

73
00:05:02,412 --> 00:05:07,147
So a typing error in the name gives you
an unprotected environment, not an error.

74
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:12,610
Plan gates. Check these before you design
a gate. Required reviewers, wait timers,

75
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:17,331
custom rules and disabling the administrator
bypass are available in public

76
00:05:17,371 --> 00:05:21,612
repositories on any plan, and in
private repositories on Enterprise.

77
00:05:21,652 --> 00:05:25,831
They aren't available in private
repositories on Free, Pro or Team.

78
00:05:25,871 --> 00:05:31,119
Deployment branch and tag rules and environment
secrets: public on any plan, private

79
00:05:31,159 --> 00:05:32,819
on Pro, Team or Enterprise.

80
00:05:33,159 --> 00:05:38,115
Whether a private repository on a Free
plan can use environments at all is a

81
00:05:38,155 --> 00:05:43,373
conflict inside GitHub's own material. A
changelog entry of the fifteenth of May

82
00:05:43,413 --> 00:05:48,040
2025 says environments are available
on all plans in public and private

83
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:53,562
repositories. The managing-environments page
on the first of October 2026 still says

84
00:05:53,602 --> 00:05:58,558
users on Free plans can configure
environments only for public repositories.

85
00:05:58,898 --> 00:06:04,220
The Phase 0 report couldn't resolve it.
The practice repository of the labs is

86
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:09,789
public, so the labs aren't affected. For
a private repository, test it before you

87
00:06:09,829 --> 00:06:10,820
promise a gate.

88
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:16,095
Inside .git. Nothing. An environment, its
rules, its secrets and the deployment

89
00:06:16,135 --> 00:06:21,070
records it produces are GitHub objects.
The only trace in Git is the word after

90
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:25,982
environment: in the workflow file. That's
why a repository that is mirrored to

91
00:06:26,022 --> 00:06:31,272
another host loses its gates, and why the
YAML line proves nothing until you've read

92
00:06:31,312 --> 00:06:32,972
the environment's settings.

93
00:06:33,312 --> 00:06:37,356
Creating environments. The documented
path in the interface is the repository's

94
00:06:37,396 --> 00:06:41,595
settings, then Environments. The same can
be done with the REST API, which is what

95
00:06:41,635 --> 00:06:44,283
you want for configuration
that is reviewed as code.

96
00:06:44,623 --> 00:06:49,689
The two reading commands are 🟢 SAFE. gh
secret set and gh variable set are 🟡

97
00:06:49,729 --> 00:06:55,064
CAUTION: they overwrite the stored value,
and the old secret value can't be read

98
00:06:55,104 --> 00:07:00,506
back. gh secret set without --body reads
the value from standard input or prompts

99
00:07:00,546 --> 00:07:06,016
for it, which keeps it out of your shell
history. Never put a real secret value on

100
00:07:06,056 --> 00:07:06,486
screen.

101
00:07:06,826 --> 00:07:11,810
The PUT is 🔴 DANGEROUS. Five answers. What
it changes: it replaces the environment's

102
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:16,775
protection settings with the body sent.
What it can destroy: the previous settings,

103
00:07:16,815 --> 00:07:21,320
required reviewers included, which exist
nowhere else unless you saved them.

104
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:24,550
How to preview: read the
environment with a GET first.

105
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:27,780
How to recover: send the
previous configuration again.

106
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:31,249
When it's appropriate: for
environment rules kept as code.

107
00:07:31,589 --> 00:07:33,851
Workflow 8: deploying to staging.

108
00:07:34,191 --> 00:07:38,892
The build job installs from the lock file,
runs the tests, builds, and uploads the

109
00:07:38,932 --> 00:07:39,413
artifact.

110
00:07:39,753 --> 00:07:44,599
The deploy-staging job needs build, names
the environment staging with a URL taken

111
00:07:44,639 --> 00:07:49,367
from a variable, checks out the repository,
downloads the artifact, and runs the

112
00:07:49,407 --> 00:07:51,751
deployment script with the token in env.

113
00:07:52,091 --> 00:07:55,309
The textbook names four decisions
worth defending in a review.

114
00:07:55,649 --> 00:08:00,373
Build once, deploy what was built. The
deploy job doesn't run the build again.

115
00:08:00,413 --> 00:08:05,382
It downloads the artifact. Every job starts
on a fresh machine, so the artifact is

116
00:08:05,422 --> 00:08:10,512
the only way the bytes travel, and it's also
the point: the thing that was tested is

117
00:08:10,552 --> 00:08:15,521
the thing that is deployed. The download
action at version 8 fails by default when

118
00:08:15,561 --> 00:08:18,941
the content doesn't match the
digest recorded at upload.

119
00:08:19,281 --> 00:08:24,008
The environment belongs to the job that
deploys. Only deploy-staging names it.

120
00:08:24,048 --> 00:08:28,836
The build job can't read the deployment
token, because an environment secret is

121
00:08:28,876 --> 00:08:32,809
"only available to workflow jobs
that reference the environment".

122
00:08:32,849 --> 00:08:37,942
So test code and build scripts, including
those of your dependencies, never run in a

123
00:08:37,982 --> 00:08:40,326
job that holds a deployment credential.

124
00:08:40,666 --> 00:08:45,256
The secret reaches the script through env.
It's never interpolated into the text of

125
00:08:45,296 --> 00:08:46,038
a run command.

126
00:08:46,378 --> 00:08:51,550
And environment.url: shown with the deployment
on GitHub, taken from an environment

127
00:08:51,590 --> 00:08:54,878
variable, so the workflow
file contains no host name.

128
00:08:55,218 --> 00:08:59,685
Every job that references an environment
creates a deployment object, unless

129
00:08:59,725 --> 00:09:04,666
deployment: false is set under environment.
That form was added on the nineteenth of

130
00:09:04,706 --> 00:09:09,648
March 2026. Required reviewers and wait
timers still apply, and it can't be combined

131
00:09:09,688 --> 00:09:14,511
with custom protection rules. Use it for
a job that needs an environment's secrets

132
00:09:14,551 --> 00:09:19,255
but deploys nothing, for example an
integration test against a staging database.

133
00:09:19,595 --> 00:09:22,107
Workflow 9: promotion to production.

134
00:09:22,447 --> 00:09:24,662
deploy-production needs deploy-staging.

135
00:09:25,002 --> 00:09:29,537
It has a concurrency group named
production with cancel-in-progress: false.

136
00:09:29,577 --> 00:09:33,930
The next video explains that block.
It names the environment production.

137
00:09:33,970 --> 00:09:38,810
Its steps are the same checkout, the same
download, the same script with another

138
00:09:38,850 --> 00:09:39,359
argument.

139
00:09:39,699 --> 00:09:44,690
The two deploy jobs are textually almost
the same. The difference is on GitHub: each

140
00:09:44,730 --> 00:09:49,660
environment has a variable and a secret of
the same name with different values, and

141
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:54,332
only production has rules. The textbook
calls this the pattern to aim for: the

142
00:09:54,372 --> 00:09:58,464
workflow describes the procedure,
the environments hold what differs.

143
00:09:58,804 --> 00:10:03,758
What the reviewer sees, from the documentation.
Nothing of it was captured for the

144
00:10:03,798 --> 00:10:08,814
book, and the labels are GitHub's and may
change. The run shows the job as waiting.

145
00:10:08,854 --> 00:10:13,503
A required reviewer opens the run, chooses
to review deployments, selects the

146
00:10:13,543 --> 00:10:17,706
environment, and approves or rejects.
A rejection fails the workflow.

147
00:10:17,746 --> 00:10:21,421
A job that nobody approves fails
automatically after 30 days.

148
00:10:21,761 --> 00:10:26,495
What an approval releases. The reviewer
approves a job of a run, and a run is bound

149
00:10:26,535 --> 00:10:31,268
to one commit. The useful habit is to look
at the range between what production has

150
00:10:31,308 --> 00:10:32,706
and what the run carries.

151
00:10:33,046 --> 00:10:37,934
What the branch rule guarantees. A deployment
branch rule compares the run's ref

152
00:10:37,974 --> 00:10:42,616
with name patterns. Since the eighth of
December 2025, for runs triggered by

153
00:10:42,656 --> 00:10:47,236
pull_request events the ref that is
evaluated is refs/pull/N/merge, and for

154
00:10:47,276 --> 00:10:51,980
pull_request_target it's the default
branch. A rule "Selected branches: main"

155
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:55,861
therefore rejects pull request
runs and runs on other branches.

156
00:10:55,901 --> 00:10:59,065
It says nothing about review
of the commits on main.

157
00:10:59,105 --> 00:11:03,931
That's the job of the ruleset on main,
the named list of rules for that branch.

158
00:11:05,771 --> 00:11:06,744
A picture helps.

159
00:11:07,084 --> 00:11:12,040
Analogy, from the textbook. An environment
is the locked door of a server room with

160
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:15,170
its own key cabinet inside.
The job is a technician.

161
00:11:15,210 --> 00:11:20,105
The door has rules: someone must sign the
visitor in, the visitor must come from a

162
00:11:20,145 --> 00:11:24,981
known department, there may be a waiting
period. The keys in the cabinet are only

163
00:11:25,021 --> 00:11:27,148
reachable after the door has opened.

164
00:11:27,488 --> 00:11:32,181
The analogy breaks in one place, and it's
the place that matters in an audit: the

165
00:11:32,221 --> 00:11:36,680
door is guarded by GitHub, not by the
server room. If your cloud account also

166
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:41,004
accepts credentials from somewhere
else, the environment protects nothing.

167
00:11:41,344 --> 00:11:44,305
Now the sentence I promised,
the one to say to a CTO.

168
00:11:44,345 --> 00:11:48,835
The gate is a combination of three things.
A ruleset that forces changes to main

169
00:11:48,875 --> 00:11:52,798
through reviewed pull requests. An
environment that accepts only main.

170
00:11:52,838 --> 00:11:57,554
And a required reviewer. In the textbook's
words: remove any one and there is a path

171
00:11:57,594 --> 00:11:58,743
around the other two.

172
00:11:59,083 --> 00:12:03,598
Without the ruleset, anyone can push to
main and the branch rule is satisfied.

173
00:12:03,638 --> 00:12:08,095
Without the branch rule, a run on any
branch can ask for approval, or with no

174
00:12:08,135 --> 00:12:12,241
reviewer, deploy. Without the reviewer,
every merge deploys on its own.

175
00:12:12,581 --> 00:12:17,059
And two more conditions from section
20B.17 that sit outside all three.

176
00:12:17,099 --> 00:12:22,340
Administrators can bypass protection rules
unless that is switched off, and whoever

177
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:27,367
can edit the environment can remove the
rule. And the credential itself must be

178
00:12:27,407 --> 00:12:32,393
bound to the environment at the cloud
provider, or it also works from a laptop.

179
00:12:34,233 --> 00:12:37,605
Three boxes, and the security
argument is in the right one.

180
00:12:37,645 --> 00:12:41,942
While the production job waits, there's
no runner and there are no secrets.

181
00:12:42,282 --> 00:12:46,870
Read the root-cause line: the gate was
assumed from the YAML and never configured or

182
00:12:46,910 --> 00:12:47,366
verified.

183
00:12:49,206 --> 00:12:49,966
Into the lab.

184
00:12:50,306 --> 00:12:58,213
Step 1: the same bytes. Replay labs/run
ch20b/lab-27-2-artifact-digest.

185
00:12:58,553 --> 00:13:03,470
git archive only reads the repository, and
it writes an archive to standard output.

186
00:13:03,510 --> 00:13:07,173
The digest of a file is a
fingerprint computed from its bytes.

187
00:13:07,513 --> 00:13:10,928
Three digests. Which are
equal? Say it out loud.

188
00:13:15,768 --> 00:13:20,704
The first two are equal, because git
archive of one commit is deterministic.

189
00:13:20,744 --> 00:13:24,108
The third differs, because
v1.1.0 is another commit.

190
00:13:24,148 --> 00:13:29,346
A build tool isn't always this reproducible.
That's the reason to move the built

191
00:13:29,386 --> 00:13:31,965
file between jobs and not to rebuild it.

192
00:13:32,305 --> 00:13:36,934
One byte appended between "build" and
"deploy". The recorded digest no longer

193
00:13:36,974 --> 00:13:42,028
matches: FAILED, exit status 1. This is what
the download action's digest check does

194
00:13:42,068 --> 00:13:42,513
for you.

195
00:13:42,853 --> 00:13:46,403
And the recovery has a rule in its
comment: don't repair the file.

196
00:13:46,443 --> 00:13:48,850
Produce it again from
the commit and compare.

197
00:13:49,190 --> 00:13:56,891
Step 2: what a deployment contains. Replay
labs/run ch20b/lab-27-3-deploy-range.

198
00:13:56,931 --> 00:14:03,760
Staging runs v1.1.0. A push to main
is about to deploy the tip of main.

199
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:07,590
A two-dot range, from what is
deployed to what will be deployed.

200
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:11,727
It lists the commits reachable from the
second name and not from the first.

201
00:14:11,767 --> 00:14:14,210
What does it list here?
Make your prediction.

202
00:14:19,050 --> 00:14:22,473
Two commits and two files:
a runbook and a lock file.

203
00:14:22,513 --> 00:14:27,569
That's the question "what am I about to
ship?", answered with a range, and the

204
00:14:27,609 --> 00:14:32,012
textbook says it's worth one line
in every deployment's job summary.

205
00:14:32,052 --> 00:14:34,103
The script would deploy 57c8425.

206
00:14:34,443 --> 00:14:36,615
Step 3: going backwards.

207
00:14:36,955 --> 00:14:42,377
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Draw
three commits in a row, with v1.1.0 under

208
00:14:42,417 --> 00:14:47,319
the first and main under the last. Now
somebody re-runs an old workflow run.

209
00:14:47,359 --> 00:14:52,326
A re-run uses the commit of the original
run, and the replay imitates that by

210
00:14:52,366 --> 00:14:56,032
detaching HEAD on the old tag.
Mark HEAD on your drawing.

211
00:14:56,072 --> 00:15:01,234
What would the script deploy, and how do
you ask Git whether that is behind what

212
00:15:01,274 --> 00:15:02,534
staging already has?

213
00:15:04,874 --> 00:15:09,899
It would deploy c4b5de2, the old commit.
git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD main

214
00:15:09,939 --> 00:15:14,836
exits with 0: yes, the commit to be
deployed is an ancestor of what is there.

215
00:15:14,876 --> 00:15:18,618
And the log shows the two commits
that would be taken away.

216
00:15:18,958 --> 00:15:24,817
Compare it with your drawing. Three commits,
the tag v1.1.0 under the first, and

217
00:15:24,857 --> 00:15:26,292
main under the last.

218
00:15:26,632 --> 00:15:30,351
Now HEAD sits on the old tag,
two commits behind main.

219
00:15:30,391 --> 00:15:34,736
Section 20B.17 has a name for
this: a re-run is a time machine.

220
00:15:34,776 --> 00:15:38,494
On a deploy workflow it's a
rollback nobody announced.

221
00:15:38,834 --> 00:15:43,960
The guard is the same command with the
arguments in the other order: is what is

222
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:49,190
deployed an ancestor of what I am about
to deploy? Exit status 0 means forwards.

223
00:15:49,230 --> 00:15:54,159
A deploy script that runs that test and
refuses on any other status can't go

224
00:15:54,199 --> 00:15:57,690
backwards by accident. Exercise
27.4 has you write it.

225
00:15:58,030 --> 00:16:03,260
Step 4: what an approval releases. Replay
labs/run ch20b/lab-27-4-promotion.

226
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:08,184
Two refs under refs/deployed/ stand
in for GitHub's deployment records.

227
00:16:08,224 --> 00:16:11,998
They're the author's bookkeeping
for the demonstration.

228
00:16:12,038 --> 00:16:16,020
GitHub keeps deployments as
platform objects, not as refs.

229
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:20,691
Production is at c4b5de2,
staging at 57c8425.

230
00:16:21,031 --> 00:16:24,942
An approval of the production job would
release the two commits between them.

231
00:16:24,982 --> 00:16:28,072
That's what a reviewer should
read before choosing "approve".

232
00:16:28,412 --> 00:16:31,283
Step 5: the hotfix that wants a shortcut.

233
00:16:31,623 --> 00:16:36,913
An urgent fix on a branch. The rule on
production says: selected branches, main.

234
00:16:37,253 --> 00:16:42,233
A branch rule looks at one fact. Which
of these commands computes that fact?

235
00:16:42,273 --> 00:16:43,290
Say it out loud.

236
00:16:48,130 --> 00:16:52,391
The ref is refs/heads/hotfix/lead-days,
so the comparison with refs/heads/main

237
00:16:52,431 --> 00:16:56,581
fails. The second test shows that the
commit isn't contained in main either.

238
00:16:56,621 --> 00:17:00,826
The branch rule looks at the first fact
only: the name of the ref the run was

239
00:17:00,866 --> 00:17:01,488
started for.

240
00:17:01,828 --> 00:17:06,533
The comment in the transcript is the
policy: the fix reaches production the way

241
00:17:06,573 --> 00:17:11,338
everything else does, through main. git
merge --no-ff is 🟡 CAUTION: it moves the

242
00:17:11,378 --> 00:17:12,239
current branch.

243
00:17:12,579 --> 00:17:17,277
Before the merge, HEAD is on the hotfix
branch, and the fix, e39e6de, isn't

244
00:17:17,317 --> 00:17:18,414
contained in main.

245
00:17:18,754 --> 00:17:23,622
After the merge the ref is main, the
hotfix is an ancestor, and the range from

246
00:17:23,662 --> 00:17:28,216
production to main now lists four
commits, with the merge fe34a26 on top.

247
00:17:28,556 --> 00:17:33,050
On your practice repository, in your
normal shell. The interface changes.

248
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:36,776
The lab text and the linked
documentation are the reference.

249
00:17:36,816 --> 00:17:41,559
No GitHub output was captured by the
authors. Never show a real secret value.

250
00:17:41,899 --> 00:17:49,049
Lab 27.3. Create the staging environment
before you add workflow 8, set its variable

251
00:17:49,089 --> 00:17:56,069
and its secret with a made-up value, and
read both back with the listing commands.

252
00:17:56,109 --> 00:18:03,088
Then add the workflow. After the push to
main, open the run: find the two jobs and

253
00:18:03,128 --> 00:18:09,765
the order between them. Open the repository's
list of deployments and find the

254
00:18:09,805 --> 00:18:13,532
record for staging with
the commit it names.

255
00:18:13,872 --> 00:18:18,538
Lab 27.4. Add the reviewer and the
branch rule to production, then read the

256
00:18:18,578 --> 00:18:21,989
environment back before you
rely on it. Add workflow 9.

257
00:18:22,029 --> 00:18:27,260
After the next push to main, open the run
and find the production job in its waiting

258
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:32,342
state. Before you approve, run the range
command in your clone and say aloud what

259
00:18:32,382 --> 00:18:37,425
you are approving. Then approve, and
afterwards try the lab's failure case, a run

260
00:18:37,465 --> 00:18:40,186
from another branch,
and see where it stops.

261
00:18:42,026 --> 00:18:43,674
Five mistakes to watch for.

262
00:18:44,014 --> 00:18:47,085
Assuming a gate from the line
environment: production.

263
00:18:47,125 --> 00:18:51,924
Root cause: protection rules are properties
of the environment object on GitHub, and

264
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:55,208
naming a missing environment
creates it without any rule.

265
00:18:55,548 --> 00:19:00,594
A misspelled environment name. Root cause:
the misspelling names a new, unprotected

266
00:19:00,634 --> 00:19:02,494
environment; there is no error.

267
00:19:02,834 --> 00:19:07,528
Rebuilding in the deploy job. Root cause:
a second build is not guaranteed to

268
00:19:07,568 --> 00:19:11,832
produce the same bytes, so the deployed
thing is not the tested thing.

269
00:19:12,172 --> 00:19:17,361
Re-running an old deploy run. Root cause:
a re-run uses the commit of the original

270
00:19:17,401 --> 00:19:19,721
run, so it deploys old code over new.

271
00:19:20,061 --> 00:19:23,552
Treating the environment as
the only production control.

272
00:19:23,592 --> 00:19:28,785
Root cause: it is enforced by GitHub, and
credentials that also work from elsewhere

273
00:19:28,825 --> 00:19:29,416
bypass it.

274
00:19:31,256 --> 00:19:36,216
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case:
a team serving an LLM application keeps

275
00:19:36,256 --> 00:19:41,339
two cloud roles. One can update the staging
service, and one can update production.

276
00:19:41,379 --> 00:19:45,783
The production role's credentials are
reachable only from the production

277
00:19:45,823 --> 00:19:50,289
environment, which requires one approval
from the on-call group, prevents

278
00:19:50,329 --> 00:19:52,388
self-review and accepts only main.

279
00:19:52,728 --> 00:19:56,826
An engineer edits a workflow on a
feature branch to "quickly deploy".

280
00:19:56,866 --> 00:20:00,124
The job fails the branch rule
before any runner starts.

281
00:20:00,164 --> 00:20:04,802
The textbook's remark: the gate did its
work without anybody reading the diff.

282
00:20:05,142 --> 00:20:10,372
And the next layer, which Part 7 covers:
with federated credentials the same idea is

283
00:20:10,412 --> 00:20:15,203
enforced by the cloud provider as well,
because the token's subject names the

284
00:20:15,243 --> 00:20:15,955
environment.

285
00:20:17,795 --> 00:20:23,331
Your turn. Do Lab 27.3, "Deploy
to staging (workflow 8)", in

286
00:20:23,371 --> 00:20:27,048
lab-manual/m27-build-package-deliver.md.

287
00:20:27,388 --> 00:20:31,617
Before the push that starts the deployment,
run the range from the last deployed

288
00:20:31,657 --> 00:20:35,833
commit to the tip of main and write down
which commits and files the deployment

289
00:20:35,873 --> 00:20:40,209
contains. Then predict what will exist on
GitHub after the run: how many jobs, how

290
00:20:40,249 --> 00:20:42,557
many artifacts, and
which deployment record.

291
00:20:42,897 --> 00:20:49,035
The challenge is Exercise 27.2, "A
deployment with five flaws", in

292
00:20:49,075 --> 00:20:52,592
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

293
00:20:56,432 --> 00:21:00,674
Answer out loud. A strong answer is a
list, and every item is a GitHub object or

294
00:21:00,714 --> 00:21:05,009
setting, not a line of YAML: that the
environment exists under exactly that name,

295
00:21:05,049 --> 00:21:09,291
which rules it has, what plan and visibility
make those rules available, who can

296
00:21:09,331 --> 00:21:13,519
bypass and who can edit them, where the
credential lives, and what protects the

297
00:21:13,559 --> 00:21:15,179
branch the environment accepts.

298
00:21:15,519 --> 00:21:19,982
Say how you would read each item back as
evidence. The follow-up assumes all of it

299
00:21:20,022 --> 00:21:23,497
is in place and asks for the
remaining ways around the approval.

300
00:21:23,537 --> 00:21:27,891
Think about re-runs, about who can change
the environment, and about credentials

301
00:21:27,931 --> 00:21:29,539
that work from somewhere else.

302
00:21:31,379 --> 00:21:32,306
Let's land this.

303
00:21:32,646 --> 00:21:34,306
You should now be able to say:

304
00:21:34,646 --> 00:21:39,811
An environment is a GitHub object; its
rules must pass before the job is sent to a

305
00:21:39,851 --> 00:21:43,175
runner, and its secrets are
readable only after that.

306
00:21:43,515 --> 00:21:48,624
Naming an environment that does not exist
creates it without rules, so a gate must

307
00:21:48,664 --> 00:21:51,827
be configured and read back,
not assumed from YAML.

308
00:21:52,167 --> 00:21:56,735
Build once and pass the artifact: the
bytes that were tested are the bytes that

309
00:21:56,775 --> 00:21:58,252
ship, checked by a digest.

310
00:21:58,592 --> 00:22:02,934
What a deployment contains is the two-dot
range from the deployed commit to the new

311
00:22:02,974 --> 00:22:07,263
one, and a deploy script should refuse
unless the old commit is an ancestor of the

312
00:22:07,303 --> 00:22:07,474
new.

313
00:22:07,814 --> 00:22:12,718
The gate is a ruleset on main, an environment
that accepts only main, and a required

314
00:22:12,758 --> 00:22:16,602
reviewer; remove any one and there
is a path around the other two.

315
00:22:18,442 --> 00:22:25,757
Read sections 20B.1 to 20B.4 of Chapter
20B. Do Lab 27.4, "Staging, then production

316
00:22:25,797 --> 00:22:32,580
behind an approval (workflow 9)", in
lab-manual/m27-build-package-deliver.md.

317
00:22:32,620 --> 00:22:39,580
Then do Exercise 27.1, "An environment,
read back", and Exercise 27.4, "A guard

318
00:22:39,620 --> 00:22:45,516
against going backwards", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

319
00:22:45,856 --> 00:22:50,923
You can now say where a production gate
really lives, and how to read it back as

320
00:22:50,963 --> 00:22:55,391
evidence. Try the range command once
before your next real deployment.

321
00:22:55,431 --> 00:23:00,688
Next time: concurrency groups, reusable
workflows, composite actions, and container

322
00:23:00,728 --> 00:23:04,582
actions. Until then, look at the
